# L §5.11 step 4: "beats is not transitive: if a beats b and b beats c, an applicable a defeats c only if it says
# beats c too. One rule can't win with the help of another (there is no team defeat)."  k1: a applies (and beats
# b, whose when is then skipped, step 3); c is beaten by no applicable rule: a and c are candidates, RUN-CONFLICT.
# k2: a doesn't apply, b applies and beats c: b is the one candidate, value -1.  (Inline figures, L §7.1.)
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
index ks = [k1, k2]
input fa[k in ks]: bool
input fb[k in ks]: bool

def x[k in ks]: int at reader
  rule a beats b = 1 when fa[k]
  rule b beats c = -1 when fb[k]
  rule c = 0

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  fa[k1] = true
  fa[k2] = false
  fb[*] = true

ask q1: x[k] for k in ks law 2026-06-30
=== expect
q1 (k = k1): refused: RUN-CONFLICT ...
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-01-01
q1 (k = k2): -1
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-01-01
